    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The assembly of the targeted ribonucleoprotein complex, comprising the catalytically active, modified Cas9 enzyme and a precisely designed single-guide RNA (sgRNA), was facilitated by AAV9 tropism for efficient transduction of hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells (HSPCs). Electroporation was employed as the primary delivery modality to maximize the intracellular concentration of the Cas9-sgRNA assembly within the HSPC cytoplasm. Subsequently, the modified cells were cultured under defined bioreactor conditions supplemented with specific growth factors and cytokines to promote polyclonal expansion and clonal maintenance. This controlled *ex vivo* manipulation facilitates the kinetics required for robust homology-directed repair (HDR) or non-homologous end joining (NHEJ) at the endogenous HBB gene locus prior to autologous reconstitution.",
      "source": "target",
