    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Initial transduction involved titrating AAV9 vectors to achieve optimal vector genome packaging density, targeting HSPCs via specific capsid receptor binding to facilitate endosomal escape and nuclear import of the Cas9 expression cassette. The selection of the modified Cas9 variant was predicated on its hyper-fidelity profile, minimizing off-target cleavage events across the genome. Subsequently, for precise gene correction, the complex was delivered via high-voltage electroporation, which transiently permeabilizes the plasma membrane via localized pore formation, enabling efficient cytosolic uptake of the pre-formed ribonucleoprotein complex.\n\nFollowing electroporation, HSPCs were immediately transferred to specialized microcarrier culture systems within a dynamic bioreactor environment. This environment was rigorously controlled for nutrient perfusion and supplemented with defined cytokine cocktails (e.g., FLT3L, TPO) to drive lineage-specific expansion while maintaining stemness characteristics. The molecular processing phase leverages the specific PAM recognition sequence by the Cas9 enzyme to induce a DNA double-strand break (DSB) at the intended HBB locus. The subsequent repair mechanism, whether precise HDR utilizing an exogenous repair template or error-prone NHEJ, dictates the downstream therapeutic outcome.\n\nFinally, post-expansion cell viability and editing efficiency were assessed through high-",
      "source": "target",
